{
  "title": "Python Break and Continue",
  "dayLabel": "Day 16 / 100",
  "scenes": [
    {
      "id": 1,
      "narration": "Ever wanted to stop a loop early or skip one specific step? In Python, we use break and continue to control our loops like a pro.",
      "remotion": {
        "type": "TitleCard",
        "props": {
          "title": "Break vs Continue",
          "subtitle": "Master Loop Control",
          "badge": "DAY 16 • PYTHON",
          "tags": [
            "Python",
            "Loops",
            "Beginner"
          ]
        },
        "chapter": "M2 Control Flow",
        "keyIdea": "Control how loops behave"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "narration": "Think of a security line. Break is like closing the gate and stopping everyone. Continue is like telling one person to skip ahead while the rest wait.",
      "remotion": {
        "type": "ComparisonCard",
        "props": {
          "title": "The Big Difference",
          "leftTitle": "Break",
          "leftPoints": [
            "Stops the loop entirely",
            "Exits immediately",
            "No more turns"
          ],
          "rightTitle": "Continue",
          "rightPoints": [
            "Skips the current turn",
            "Moves to next item",
            "Loop keeps going"
          ]
        },
        "popups": [
          {
            "anchor": "gate",
            "text": "Break = Exit",
            "kind": "tip"
          },
          {
            "anchor": "skip",
            "text": "Continue = Skip",
            "kind": "note"
          }
        ],
        "keyIdea": "Break exits, Continue skips"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "narration": "Look at this loop. We check for the number three. When we hit it, break stops everything. The loop ends right there, printing only one and two.",
      "remotion": {
        "type": "TypingCode",
        "type_anchor": "Look",
        "props": {
          "filename": "break_demo.py",
          "language": "python",
          "code": "for i in range(1, 6):\n    if i == 3:\n        break\n    print(i)",
          "highlightLines": [
            3
          ]
        },
        "camera": [
          {
            "at": "break",
            "zoom": 1.7,
            "x": 50,
            "y": 30
          }
        ],
        "popups": [
          {
            "anchor": "stops",
            "text": "Stops the entire loop",
            "kind": "tip"
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "narration": "Now watch continue. If the number is three, we skip the print command and jump straight to the next number, four. Only three is missing from the output.",
      "remotion": {
        "type": "TypingCode",
        "type_anchor": "Now",
        "props": {
          "filename": "continue_demo.py",
          "language": "python",
          "code": "for i in range(1, 6):\n    if i == 3:\n        continue\n    print(i)",
          "highlightLines": [
            3
          ]
        },
        "camera": [
          {
            "at": "continue",
            "zoom": 1.7,
            "x": 50,
            "y": 30
          }
        ],
        "popups": [
          {
            "anchor": "skip",
            "text": "Skips just this turn",
            "kind": "note"
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "narration": "Break kills the loop. Continue skips a turn. Try using break to find a specific name in a list! Check the channel for the full lesson.",
